        "heading": "What Counts as Enough Evidence",
        "content": "The single most common failure mode among new analysts is confusing a feeling with a finding. 'I think this is fine' and 'I can show you why this is fine' are completely different statements, and only the second one should let you close an alert. The discipline of evidence sufficiency starts with a simple internal check: if my team lead asked me right now to justify this decision in one sentence, citing a specific field and value, could I do it? If the honest answer is no — if your justification is a shrug, a hunch, or 'it's probably nothing' — you do not yet have enough evidence to walk through the close door, no matter how confident you feel.\n\n### The Asymmetric Cost of Error\n\nThe evidence bar, importantly, is not symmetric between closing and escalating, and understanding why is central to good judgment. Closing a genuinely malicious alert as a false positive means an active intrusion continues undetected, potentially for days or weeks, until it resurfaces as a much larger and more expensive incident — a ransomware deployment, a data breach notification, a call from a customer's security team asking why your infrastructure is scanning theirs.\n\nEscalating a false positive, by contrast, costs a Tier 2 analyst or an on-call lead a few minutes of review and mild annoyance if it turns out to be nothing. Those two error costs are wildly different in magnitude, and a rational analyst calibrates their confidence threshold accordingly: you should require considerably stronger evidence to close an alert than you require to escalate one.\n\nThis is not encouragement to escalate everything reflexively — that just moves the queue-management problem onto someone else and erodes trust in your judgment over time — but when you are genuinely on the fence, the asymmetry should tip you toward escalation, not closure.\n\n### Evidence to Close vs. Escalate\n\nWhat actually constitutes sufficient evidence differs by the kind of claim you are making. To close an alert, you typically need a positive, specific reason the activity is expected:\n\n- A matching change ticket.\n- A documented recurring scheduled task with a consistent historical pattern.\n- A known and inventoried admin tool used by a known account from a known workstation.\n- A source IP that maps to your own vulnerability scanner.\n\nAbsence of obvious badness is not the same as presence of a good explanation — 'I did not find anything alarming' after a five-minute look is a much weaker basis for closure than 'I found the specific, documented reason this occurred.'\n\nTo escalate, the bar is lower by design: you need one or two corroborating signals that raise real concern, even if you cannot yet build the full story. A process you do not recognize, running with a parent-child relationship that does not fit your baseline, followed by an outbound connection to an IP with no clean reputation history, is enough to escalate even though you have not proven intrusion. Escalation is a request for more eyes and more authority, not a verdict.\n\n### Turn Worry Into a Query\n\nWhen you find yourself genuinely stuck between closing and escalating, the productive move is not to keep staring at the same evidence hoping it resolves itself — it is to ask a sharper, more specific question that a targeted query can actually answer. 'Is this suspicious?' is not answerable by any single query; it is too vague to act on. 'Has this exact process-parent-child relationship occurred on this host before, and if so, how often?' is answerable in one pivot and often collapses the ambiguity immediately. Reframing a vague worry into a specific, checkable question is, in practice, the single highest-leverage habit a SOC analyst can build, because it turns paralysis into a concrete next action almost every time.\n\n### Two Analysts, Same Door\n\nConsider two analysts looking at the identical alert: an RDP logon to a finance server from an internal IP outside business hours. Analyst A closes it, reasoning 'RDP happens all the time, probably an admin.' Analyst B pulls the logon history for that account against that host over the last ninety days, finds this exact account has RDP'd into this exact server every other Tuesday night for six months matching a documented backup-verification task, and closes it with that citation attached.\n\nBoth analysts reached the same door. Only one of them produced evidence that would survive a second look — and if this had turned out to be the one time in fifty that pattern was actually an attacker riding a compromised credential, only Analyst B's note would let anyone reconstruct what was checked and why it was trusted.",
        "codeExample": "// Evidence-sufficiency check — apply before walking through Door 1 or Door 2\n//\n// BEFORE YOU CLOSE, you must be able to complete this sentence with a\n// SPECIFIC field + value, not a feeling:\n//   \"I am closing this because ___________________________.\"\n//   GOOD:  \"...TargetUserName=svc_backup RDP'd to this host every other\n//           Tuesday for 6 months per logon history, matching change\n//           ticket CHG-4471 (documented recurring backup verification).\"\n//   BAD:   \"...it's probably just an admin doing admin stuff.\"\n//\n// BEFORE YOU ESCALATE, the bar is intentionally lower — one or two\n// corroborating signals is enough, you are NOT required to prove intrusion:\n//   GOOD:  \"Unrecognized parent-child process pair not seen in 90-day\n//           baseline for this host, PLUS outbound connection to an IP\n//           with no clean reputation history. Requesting Tier 2 review.\"\n//\n// ASYMMETRIC COST TABLE (why the two bars are different heights):\n//   Wrongly CLOSE a true positive  -> intrusion continues undetected,\n//                                      resurfaces bigger and costlier later\n//   Wrongly ESCALATE a false pos.  -> a few minutes of a reviewer's time\n//   => require MORE evidence to close than to escalate.\n//\n// STUCK BETWEEN DOORS? Reframe the vague worry into one answerable query:\n//   VAGUE (unanswerable):  \"Is this suspicious?\"\n//   SHARP (one pivot away): \"Has this exact process/parent pair occurred\n//                            on this host before, and how often?\""
